      "Central banks are holding a smaller percentage of US Treasury debt. The explanation provided is that the market has grown so large that their share has naturally diluted, rather than reflecting a loss of confidence.",
      "The Poison:\nBy framing this as a simple math problem of 'market expansion,' the analysis ignores the underlying fiscal policy that necessitates such massive debt issuance.",
      "The Breakdown & Plane Error:\nThis is a CAUSE/HOW error. The analysis treats the expansion of debt as an independent variable (HOW) rather than a consequence of fiscal policy (CAUSE).",
      "The Trajectory: The Path of Deception.\nWhen you map the gap between the stated neutral observation and the actual fiscal reality...",
      "...it plots a direct trajectory toward Lesser Evil. The system is actively forcing a shift in risk that is being masked by technical jargon.",
      "The Unavoidable Truth: Debt expansion is not a neutral market event; it is a policy choice with systemic consequences.\n\nThe Unavoidable Lie: That the dilution of central bank holdings is merely a byproduct of market size.",
      "Alethekanon:\nThe analysis fails to account for the active force of fiscal policy. By treating the Treasury market as an autonomous actor, it obscures the reality of systemic debt accumulation.",
      "Awwthekanon:\nIt is worrying to see such complex financial shifts explained away so coldly. Behind these numbers are real risks to the stability of the economy that affect everyone's future.",
      "Brothekanon:\n'It's just the market getting bigger, bro.' Sure, and my credit card debt is just my 'spending capacity' getting bigger. Same energy. This is just a fancy way of saying we're printing too much."
