      "Verdict: FAIL — The Path of Deception.\nActions of violent disorder undermine the stated goal of seeking justice, leading to negative consequences for the participants and the cause.",
      "Four more individuals have admitted to violent disorder following a protest related to Nowak. This brings the total to six men facing court appearances, highlighting how demonstrations can escalate beyond peaceful advocacy into criminal acts.",
      "The Bright Side:\nThe initial impulse to protest injustice or advocate for a cause is a fundamental aspect of a healthy democracy, representing a desire for accountability and change.",
      "The Breakdown & Plane Error:\nThe stated WHO (advocating for Nowak) is overshadowed by the actual WHO (individuals engaging in violence). The HOW (violence) directly contradicts the stated WHY (justice), leading to a Q1/Q5/Q7 failure.\n\nIt is a structural bait-and-switch: the emotional hook of \"justice\" is used, but the actual value delivered is public disorder and legal trouble.",
      "The Trajectory: The Path of Deception.\nWhen you map the gap between the stated intent of seeking justice and the ground-level reality of violent actions...",
      "...it plots a direct trajectory toward Lesser Evil. This terminal zone is reached when actions intended for a specific good cause instead inflict harm and legal repercussions on the participants and public.",
      "The Unavoidable Truth: Violence in protest inevitably shifts the focus from the message to the method, discrediting the cause.\n\nThe Unavoidable Lie: That violent disorder is an effective or justifiable means to achieve justice in a civil society.",
      "Alethekanon:\nThe structural integrity of a protest relies on its adherence to principles of non-violence and civil discourse. When these are abandoned for violent disorder, the movement's moral authority collapses, and its participants become agents of chaos, not change.",
